---
// ===========================================================================
// showcase.astro — the example, running, for somebody who cannot run it.
//
// The Examples page listed six commands, which is useful to a reader who has
// the repository and useless to the one this site is for. A documentation site
// that describes a form library and shows no form is asking to be taken on
// trust twice: once for the prose, and once for the claim that the prose
// describes something real.
//
// So the biggest example is ON this page rather than beside it, built from the
// same files the command serves — and the same files `npm run test:types`
// compiles, so it cannot quietly stop being what the page says it is.
//
// `client:load` rather than `client:visible`, and the reason is what the page
// is. A reader who opened /showcase/ came for the form; deferring the only
// thing on the page until they scroll buys nothing and costs the one thing
// that matters — measured here, the island sat at 728x2211 spanning the
// viewport with its `ssr` attribute still set, so the inputs rendered with
// every attribute off the schema and nothing was listening to them. A form
// that looks complete and ignores typing is worse than one that is late.
// ===========================================================================
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import ShowcaseScreen from "../demo/ShowcaseScreen.tsx";
import AutoScreen from "../demo/auto/AutoScreen.tsx";
import autoSchemaSource from "../demo/auto/signup-form.ts?raw";
import autoWidgetsSource from "../demo/auto/signup-widgets.tsx?raw";
import autoScreenSource from "../demo/auto/AutoScreen.tsx?raw";
import { SOURCE, FILE_COUNT, LINE_COUNT } from "../demo/showcase-source.ts";
import { SECTIONS } from "../demo/showcase-sections.ts";
import { panelIdOf } from "../demo/section-panel.ts";
---

<Base
  title="Showcase — waypoint"
  description="A real form running on this page: six sections, every label, bound and aria attribute off the schema, and a submit that names what blocked it."
>
  <h1>Showcase</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    A trade account application — six sections, an address written once and
    placed twice, an order table with a cross-field ceiling, and a submit that
    tells you what stopped it. It is running on this page.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">The schema supplies labels, constraints, and accessibility attributes. The application supplies the widgets.</p>

  <p class="caption">Submit empty to see blocking paths. Toggle <em>Same as billing</em> to retain shipping values while excluding their errors. Exceed the order ceiling to see an error on <code>items</code>.</p>

  <!-- THE GUEST IS NAMED, AND IN THE SITE'S OWN GRAMMAR. Every other block of
       evidence on this site arrives under `qualifier: value` — `file:`,
       `shell:`, `app:` — and the one block a reader can actually type into was
       the only one that arrived unannounced. Code.astro cannot label this one:
       the strip it draws lives inside the block, and this block is somebody
       else's application with a border and a radius of its own.

       Flush, with no gap of its own, for the same reason the code strip is
       flush: a label spaced evenly between the paragraph above and the screen
       below belongs to neither. The screen draws its own frame in
       ShowcaseScreen.tsx, so nothing is drawn around it here — a second border
       would frame a framed thing. -->
  <p class="label">
    <span class="qualifies">app</span><span class="colon">:</span>{" "}
    <span class="named">showcase</span>
  </p>

  <ShowcaseScreen client:load />

  <!-- ONE PANEL PER SECTION, opened by the Code control in that section's own
       header. The listing at the foot of this page answers "what is all of
       this"; a reader standing in front of the order lines has a narrower
       question, and walking them down to a file named in a group is not an
       answer to it.

       They live out here rather than inside the island because the code in
       them is highlighted at build time, and because a popover is put in the
       top layer by the browser — where it sits in the document decides
       nothing about where it appears. -->
  {
    SECTIONS.map((section) => (
      <div id={panelIdOf(section.id)} popover class="source-panel">
        <!-- A click outside closes it already — that is the browser's own
             light dismiss, and it was checked here rather than assumed. The
             button is for the reader who does not know that, and for the one
             whose pointer is a finger. -->
        <button
          type="button"
          class="source-panel-close"
          popovertarget={panelIdOf(section.id)}
          popovertargetaction="hide"
          aria-label={`Close the code for ${section.title}`}>&times;</button
        >
        <p class="label">
          <span class="qualifies">file</span>
          <span class="colon">:</span>{" "}
          <span class="named">{section.file}</span>
        </p>
        <h2 class="source-panel-title">{section.title}</h2>
        <p class="caption">{section.note}</p>
        <Code code={section.code} lang="tsx" flush />
      </div>
    ))
  }

  <!-- A SHOWCASE THAT SHOWS NO SOURCE IS A DEMO. The form ran here and the
       page said, in prose, that every label and bound came off the schema —
       which is a claim about code, made without the code. What a form library
       is selling is what the calling code looks like, so the calling code is
       the exhibit, and a listing with parts left out is worth about as much as
       no listing: the reader cannot tell what the missing part was doing. -->
  <h2>Every file behind it</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    All {FILE_COUNT} files of <code>examples/showcase</code>, {LINE_COUNT}{" "}
    lines, as they are on disk. The screen above imports this directory
    directly, so this is the source of the form you just typed into rather than
    a listing beside it — and the build fails if the directory gains a file
    that is not on this page.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    The two groups that hold every call into the library are open. The widgets
    and the build files are folded, named and counted, one click each — with
    all twenty-one unfolded the page is forty screens of code and a reader
    cannot get past it.
  </p>

  {
    SOURCE.map((group) => (
      <>
        <h3>{group.title}</h3>
        <p class="caption">{group.note}</p>
        {group.files.map((source) => (
          <details class="source-file" open={group.openByDefault}>
            <summary class="label">
              <span class="qualifies">file</span>
              <span class="colon">:</span>
              <span class="named">{source.path}</span>
              <span class="lines">{source.lines}</span>
            </summary>
            <Code code={source.code} lang={source.lang} flush />
          </details>
        ))}
      </>
    ))
  }

  <!-- THE OTHER END OF THE DIAL, and it was a name in a list until now.
       `<AutoForm>` sat in the API index with one sentence under it and no
       output anywhere on this site — a reader met a top-level component of
       this library for the first time as a signature, with nothing to look at
       and no way to tell what it would put on their screen.

       It gets its own schema rather than the showcase's on purpose: the
       showcase's schema declares no titles, so drawing it this way produces a
       column of unlabelled inputs, which demonstrates the descriptor contract
       and nothing a reader wants. Titles are where the label comes from, and
       a demo of layer 1 has to be a schema that carries them. -->
  <!-- An explicit id, because the link to it comes from /api/ and the rail
       mints ids in the browser: a reference row pointing at an anchor that
       only exists once JavaScript has run is a link that works for the
       reader who did not need it. -->
  <h2 id="autoform">The same library, with no component code</h2>

  <p class="lead">
    Everything above is layer 3 — a component per field, written by hand.
    Layer 1 is the other end: <code>&lt;AutoForm /&gt;</code> walks the
    registered fields in declaration order and draws each one through a table
    of widgets. The form below has no component of its own for any field, and
    no path written anywhere in it.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">Things to try.</strong> Empty the name and
    leave the field: the complaint arrives under it and the count in the bar
    moves. Put 900 in Seats — the limit is in the schema, not in the widget.
    The plan's three options are not listed in any component either; a closed
    field carries its own choices and that is what routed it to a select.
  </p>

  <p class="label">
    <span class="qualifies">app</span><span class="colon">:</span>{" "}
    <span class="named">signup, drawn by AutoForm</span>
  </p>

  <!-- `client:load`, and for the reason recorded at the top of this file
       rather than a new one: written `client:visible` it was served with its
       `ssr` attribute still set and never hydrated, so the inputs carried
       every attribute off the schema and nothing was listening to them — the
       same failure the showcase island hit, on the same page, caught here
       because the pass this screen runs at mount never ran. -->
  <AutoScreen client:load />

  <p class="caption">
    That is three files and no fourth. The schema declares the fields and their
    titles, four widgets say what an input of each kind looks like, and the
    screen wires them together — add a field to the schema and it appears,
    labelled, validated and blocking the submit, with none of the three edited.
  </p>

  <details class="source-file" open>
    <summary class="label">
      <span class="qualifies">file</span>
      <span class="colon">:</span>
      <span class="named">signup-form.ts</span>
      <span class="lines">{autoSchemaSource.trimEnd().split("\n").length}</span>
    </summary>
    <Code code={autoSchemaSource.trimEnd()} lang="ts" flush />
  </details>

  <details class="source-file" open>
    <summary class="label">
      <span class="qualifies">file</span>
      <span class="colon">:</span>
      <span class="named">signup-widgets.tsx</span>
      <span class="lines">{autoWidgetsSource.trimEnd().split("\n").length}</span>
    </summary>
    <Code code={autoWidgetsSource.trimEnd()} lang="tsx" flush />
  </details>

  <details class="source-file" open>
    <summary class="label">
      <span class="qualifies">file</span>
      <span class="colon">:</span>
      <span class="named">AutoScreen.tsx</span>
      <span class="lines">{autoScreenSource.trimEnd().split("\n").length}</span>
    </summary>
    <Code code={autoScreenSource.trimEnd()} lang="tsx" flush />
  </details>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>What it costs.</strong> This is the one layer where a path is not
    checked by the compiler. <code>AutoForm</code> reads paths out of the
    descriptor tree and splices row indices into them at run time, so there is
    nothing for a reader to have typed and nothing to check it against — which
    is the deal layer 1 offers, and the reason the rest of this page is layer
    3. Everything above is checked; this is drawn.
  </p>

  <h2>The same thing, locally</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    The screen above imports <code>examples/showcase</code> directly, so it is
    the example rather than a copy of it. To run it on its own — with the
    package resolving to source, so editing the runtime reloads the page —{" "}
    <code>npm run example:showcase</code>, then <code>localhost:5179</code>. The
    other five are on the
    <a class="link" href="/examples/">Examples</a> page.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    One detail worth knowing if you read its source: the showcase registers
    itself as <code>application</code>, not <code>form</code>. This site's own
    demos already register <code>form</code>, and a registry augmentation
    belongs to the whole compilation — so two files declaring one key merge,
    silently, and whichever the checker reaches last wins. Two names is what
    lets both run here, and it is what qualified paths are for.
  </p>
</Base>
